      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's another attempt at explaining mitosis in a more helpful tone:\n\nWhen a cell divides, it must go through a precise process to ensure that the new cells receive a complete set of genetic instructions. This is where mitosis comes in – a type of cell division that replicates the cell's DNA exactly, creating two identical daughter cells. Imagine you have a blueprint for your house, and you want to create an exact duplicate. You'd need to make an exact copy of the blueprint, and then use that copy to build another identical house. Mitosis is similar, but instead of a blueprint, it's working with the cell's genetic material, DNA. As a result, the new cells you get can go on to grow and develop into the same type of tissue or organism as the original cell. This is incredibly important for our bodies, as it allows us to replace old or damaged cells with new ones, helping us heal from injuries and stay healthy.",
